<Project Sdk="Microsoft.NET.Sdk">

  <!--
    _build.csproj - NUKE build entry point.

    Lives in build/ (NOT src/, apps/, or tests/) so the solution-wide
    Directory.Build.props / targets / CPM analyzers don't apply. Instead, the
    sibling build/Directory.Build.props gives NUKE a permissive environment
    (analyzers OFF, TreatWarningsAsErrors OFF), and the PropertyGroup below
    turns CPM off (it is that copy which decides — see the comment there).

    Run via the bootstrap scripts:
        ./build.sh Compile          [Linux / macOS]
        build.ps1 Compile           [Windows PowerShell]

    Or directly:
        dotnet run project=build Compile

    The Build.cs class defines targets: Clean, Restore, Compile, Test,
    ArchitectureTests, PublishCliMinimal, PublishCliFull, PublishSingleFile,
    PublishAot, PublishAvalonia, PublishBlazor, PublishAll.
  -->

  <PropertyGroup>
    <OutputType>Exe</OutputType>
    <TargetFramework>net10.0</TargetFramework>
    <IsPackable>false</IsPackable>
    <NukeTelemetryVersion>1</NukeTelemetryVersion>
    <EnableNETAnalyzers>false</EnableNETAnalyzers>
    <NoWarn>$(NoWarn);CS0649;CS0169;CS8618</NoWarn>
    <RootNamespace>Harbor.Build</RootNamespace>
    <AssemblyName>_build</AssemblyName>

    <!--
      Disable Central Package Management for THIS project only. The solution-
      wide Directory.Packages.props enables CPM; NUKE is a build tool, not
      a shipped assembly, so we let it pin its own dep chain inline.

      The consequence, written down so the next reader does not have to
      derive it: with CPM off here, THIS FILE is the only place the NUKE
      and MSBuild versions are decided. Directory.Packages.props therefore
      carries NO Nuke.Common entry — a pin there would be dead text for
      this project, and the comment beside it a second, contradictory answer
      to "which NUKE do we build with?". #1896 removed that entry for
      exactly this reason.

      Why the property is repeated HERE and not only in
      build/Directory.Build.props: the SDK imports Directory.Build.props
      first and Directory.Packages.props second (Microsoft.Common.props
      imports NuGet.props, which imports the central file), so the root
      file's ManagePackageVersionsCentrally=true lands AFTER the local
      Directory.Build.props set it to false. This PropertyGroup is
      evaluated after both imports, so the value below is the one that
      survives. Change it here.
    -->
    <ManagePackageVersionsCentrally>false</ManagePackageVersionsCentrally>
    <CentralPackageTransitivePinningEnabled>false</CentralPackageTransitivePinningEnabled>
  </PropertyGroup>

  <!--
    THE VERSIONS BELOW ARE DECIDED HERE AND NOWHERE ELSE.

    Two properties above make that true, and they are also the reason the
    pins are needed at all:

      - ManagePackageVersionsCentrally=false — no central version catalogue
        applies to this project, so a version written anywhere else would
        be ignored here.
      - CentralPackageTransitivePinningEnabled=false — nothing lifts NUKE's
        transitive tree to a patched version, so every package that has to
        leave its vulnerable version is pinned explicitly below.

    Nuke.Common 9.0.4 → 10.1.0, the current stable (published 2025-12-02).
    10.1.0 is what moves the Microsoft.Build* floor below from 17.x to
    18.0.2: Nuke.ProjectModel 10.1.0 asks for Microsoft.Build 18.0.2 from its
    net10.0 group (17.14.28 from net9.0, 17.11.48 from net8.0), and this
    project targets net10.0, so net10.0 is the group NuGet resolves.

    The pins, and what each one holds back:

      - System.Security.Cryptography.Xml 10.0.13 — KEEP THIS PIN, it is
        load-bearing. NUKE's tree pulls 9.0.0, and 9.0.0 is inside 11
        advisory ranges (GHSA-c399-9853-6jmj / CVE-2026-96959,
        GHSA-7hqp-7c5m-4vq6 / CVE-2026-96960, GHSA-r955-8q5j-3h83 /
        CVE-2026-96958, and eight more). 10.0.13 is outside every one of
        them. REMOVING THE PIN RE-OPENS ALL 11 ON THE TRANSITIVE 9.0.0 —
        the assembly the build process would then load. It also has to be
        >= 10.0.13 and not merely >= 10.0.0: the band
        >= 10.0.0, <= 10.0.12 is what 10.0.11 and 10.0.12 are still inside
        of — the same three CVEs.
      - Microsoft.Build, .Tasks.Core, .Utilities.Core 18.0.2 — 18.0.2 is the
        floor Nuke.ProjectModel 10.1.0 requires, not the newest 18.x. A
        direct pin BELOW what NUKE requires is NU1605 ("Detected package
        downgrade"), an error rather than a warning, so 17.14.28 would no
        longer restore next to NUKE 10.1.0. It is not the newest 18.x on
        purpose either: 18.10.1 and later ship net11.0 assets, which a
        net10.0 project cannot consume (NU1202). 18.0.2 closes the
        advisories the old 17.12.6 transitive carried (GHSA-w3q9-fxm7-j8fq,
        GHSA-h4j7-5rxr-p4wc) and adds none of its own.
      - NuGet.Packaging 6.12.5 — Nuke.Tooling 10.1.0 still asks for 6.12.1;
        6.12.5 sits above it (no NU1605) and closes NU1901: GHSA-g4vj-cjjj-v7hg.
  -->
  <ItemGroup>
    <PackageReference Include="Nuke.Common" Version="10.1.0"/>
    <PackageReference Include="System.Security.Cryptography.Xml" Version="10.0.13"/>
    <PackageReference Include="Microsoft.Build" Version="18.0.2"/>
    <PackageReference Include="Microsoft.Build.Tasks.Core" Version="18.0.2"/>
    <PackageReference Include="Microsoft.Build.Utilities.Core" Version="18.0.2"/>
    <PackageReference Include="NuGet.Packaging" Version="6.12.5"/>
  </ItemGroup>

</Project>
